## 🔔 Pattern 3: Observer Pattern (Decoupled Messaging)
### 📁 Files: 
* Payments: [paymentEvents.js](file:///c:/Users/admin/Desktop/Tailor%20shop/payments-service/src/events/paymentEvents.js) & [notificationHandlers.js](file:///c:/Users/admin/Desktop/Tailor%20shop/payments-service/src/services/notificationHandlers.js)
* Orders: [orderEvents.js](file:///c:/Users/admin/Desktop/Tailor%20shop/backend/src/events/orderEvents.js) & [orderObserver.js](file:///c:/Users/admin/Desktop/Tailor%20shop/backend/src/observers/orderObserver.js)

The **Observer Pattern** is a behavioral pattern where an object (the Subject) maintains a list of dependents (Observers) and automatically notifies them of state changes.

```
 [Orders Controller (Subject)]
            │
            ▼  (1. Emits STATUS_CHANGED event)
    [ orderEvents Bus ]
            │
            ├─► [ ConsoleSmsNotificationAdapter (Observer) ] ──► (Prints simulated SMS)
            │
            └─► [ EmailNotificationAdapter (Observer) ] ────────► (Sends actual Email via Resend)
```

### How the Code Executes (Step-by-Step)
1. **Bootstrapping Observers:** When the backend application boots up (`backend/src/app.js`), it executes `setupOrderObservers()`. This function hooks observers into the event bus:
   ```javascript
   orderEvents.on(ORDER_EVENTS.STATUS_CHANGED, async (payload) => { ... });
   ```
2. **State Change occurs:** A tailor updates the status of a customer suit from `'pending'` to `'in_progress'`.
3. **Event Fired:** The order controller completes the database transaction and immediately broadcasts the event along with the data payload:
   ```javascript
   orderEvents.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.STATUS_CHANGED, {
     order: orderDoc,
     customer: orderDoc.customer_id,
   });
   ```
4. **Asynchronous Notification Triggered:** The observer catches the broadcast, extracts the payload, formats a friendly message, and invokes the notification adapters asynchronously without blocking the REST controller's response to the tailor's browser.

